{"title": "Soil mineralogy", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Major", "level": "Foundational", "article": null, "definition": {"text": "The study of the minerals in soil, one of the branches, with soil physics, chemistry and biology, through which soil processes are studied quantitatively.", "sent_ids": ["e268b352a09c"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Most of the elements plants need come from the chemical breakdown of soil minerals.", "sent_ids": ["f54f2dbe7f6d"]}, {"text": "As roots remove nutrients from the soil solution, weathering of soil minerals helps replenish them, along with ion exchange and decay of organic matter.", "sent_ids": ["f6c634a7b6fb"]}, {"text": "Soil colour is mainly influenced by soil mineralogy.", "sent_ids": ["fc755e6698b5"]}, {"text": "Gypsum, calcite, feldspar or quartz grains can be clay-sized without behaving like true clay minerals.", "sent_ids": ["450d41a05978"]}, {"text": "Soil minerals retain phosphorus, by adsorption onto iron and aluminium oxyhydroxides in acid soils and precipitation onto calcite in neutral to calcareous ones.", "sent_ids": ["d7af5fbf51b8"]}, {"text": "Organic matter binds to soil minerals: hydrophobic molecules partition onto them and stay longer in soil, and particulate organic matter adheres to them.", "sent_ids": ["2cc7a07feefc", "27140b0dcc3d"]}, {"text": "Weathering of soil minerals releases salts, which drainage flushes out or evaporation concentrates near the surface.", "sent_ids": ["2dc142c67fd3"]}, {"text": "Advances in soil mineralogy, with the other soil sciences, have added new tools to the study of soil formation.", "sent_ids": ["5e48fe91c151"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-found-029", "soil-found-119", "soil-found-052"], "related": ["soil-chem-079", "soil-found-031", "soil-found-035", "soil-chem-187", "soil-found-025", "soil-chem-076"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": ["Mineralogy"], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil chemistry", "chapter": "Soil mineralogy", "id": "soil-chem-184", "score": 7.32, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 3, "tests": 1, "pages": 3.32, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "History of soil science", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_soil_science", "revid": 1372435895, "sent_ids": ["5e48fe91c151", "e268b352a09c"]}, {"title": "Botany", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Botany", "revid": 1386043892, "sent_ids": ["f54f2dbe7f6d"]}, {"title": "Dissolved organic carbon", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dissolved_organic_carbon", "revid": 1385990882, "sent_ids": ["2cc7a07feefc"]}, {"title": "Particulate organic matter", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Particulate_organic_matter", "revid": 1379700017, "sent_ids": ["27140b0dcc3d"]}, {"title": "Phosphorus cycle", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phosphorus_cycle", "revid": 1383855943, "sent_ids": ["d7af5fbf51b8"]}, {"title": "Physical properties of soil", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Physical_properties_of_soil", "revid": 1382566534, "sent_ids": ["fc755e6698b5"]}, {"title": "Plant nutrients in soil", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plant_nutrients_in_soil", "revid": 1375043680, "sent_ids": ["f6c634a7b6fb"]}, {"title": "Soil matrix", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_matrix", "revid": 1377650191, "sent_ids": ["450d41a05978"]}, {"title": "Soil salinity", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_salinity", "revid": 1371379663, "sent_ids": ["2dc142c67fd3"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-found-029", "soil-found-052"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-found-029": "ordering pass", "soil-found-119": "ordering pass", "soil-found-052": "consolidation"}, "unit": 127, "position": 3}
{"title": "Pedogenic carbonate", "kind": "Material", "weight": "Major", "level": "Intermediate", "article": null, "definition": {"text": "Carbonate that forms in place within a soil, as opposed to carbonate inherited from the bedrock or parent material.", "sent_ids": ["e70d79b71b29"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Acid hydrolysis by dissolved carbon dioxide, called carbonation, can weather primary minerals into secondary carbonate minerals.", "sent_ids": ["4ab673a6b032"]}, {"text": "Horizon descriptions in the WRB and the FAO guidelines mark accumulated secondary carbonates with the suffix k.", "sent_ids": ["33b497ddef36", "17b3ae7042fe"]}, {"text": "Secondary carbonates define WRB groups: Calcisols accumulate them, Chernozems and Kastanozems have them, Phaeozems lack them within one metre.", "sent_ids": ["a5c7523b1a47", "3e90dd818674", "0213e853a21c", "e1d04b1be9ad"]}, {"text": "Carbon isotopes in pedogenic carbonate nodules of paleosols are used to reconstruct past atmospheric carbon dioxide.", "sent_ids": ["c022258f5c18"]}, {"text": "It can form during soil wetting and drying, which underlies proposed proxies for past seasonality.", "sent_ids": ["d7ade814ccae"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-found-034", "soil-found-060", "soil-form-087"], "related": ["soil-chem-036", "soil-class-121", "soil-class-130", "soil-form-072", "soil-env-106"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil chemistry", "chapter": "Soil mineralogy", "id": "soil-chem-185", "score": 6.81, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 3, "tests": 1, "pages": 2.81, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "World Reference Base for Soil Resources", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Reference_Base_for_Soil_Resources", "revid": 1378817528, "sent_ids": ["a5c7523b1a47", "3e90dd818674", "0213e853a21c"]}, {"title": "Paleosol", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paleosol", "revid": 1307290060, "sent_ids": ["c022258f5c18", "d7ade814ccae"]}, {"title": "Soil horizon", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_horizon", "revid": 1385480933, "sent_ids": ["33b497ddef36", "17b3ae7042fe"]}, {"title": "Phaeozem", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phaeozem", "revid": 1361002814, "sent_ids": ["e1d04b1be9ad"]}, {"title": "Soil carbon", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_carbon", "revid": 1385829693, "sent_ids": ["e70d79b71b29"]}, {"title": "Weathering", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weathering", "revid": 1382411154, "sent_ids": ["4ab673a6b032"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-found-034", "soil-found-060"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-found-034": "ordering pass", "soil-found-060": "ordering pass", "soil-form-087": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 127, "position": 2}
{"title": "Bowen's reaction series", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Major", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Bowen's reaction series", "definition": {"text": "The sequence in which common silicate minerals crystallize from a cooling basaltic magma, summarized by the petrologist Norman L. Bowen.", "sent_ids": ["ab01f9d33994"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Olivine and calcium plagioclase crystallize first, followed by pyroxene, amphibole, biotite, sodium plagioclase, orthoclase, muscovite and finally quartz.", "sent_ids": ["0caf56abe9ed"]}, {"text": "It has a continuous branch of feldspars that grow more sodium-rich as temperature falls, and a discontinuous branch of mafic minerals.", "sent_ids": ["a33176b129fc", "96bf571c579b"]}, {"text": "Bowen derived it in the early 1900s by melting powdered rock and noting the minerals that formed at progressively cooler temperatures.", "sent_ids": ["438bd09c55fc", "57a509d588f1"]}, {"text": "It explains why some minerals tend to occur together and others almost never do.", "sent_ids": ["af807afd4858"]}, {"text": "Because minerals are most stable near their conditions of formation, the series also ranks resistance to weathering at Earth's surface, with the last-formed most stable.", "sent_ids": ["9dc7cc8cc0b8", "fa325d14a5a0"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-found-031", "soil-form-135", "soil-found-029"], "related": ["soil-chem-187", "soil-found-037"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil chemistry", "chapter": "Soil mineralogy", "id": "soil-chem-186", "score": 11.66, "score_parts": {"importance": 3.584, "weight": 3, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 1.5}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Bowen's reaction series", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bowen's_reaction_series", "revid": 1380152309, "sent_ids": ["a33176b129fc", "96bf571c579b", "438bd09c55fc", "57a509d588f1", "af807afd4858", "9dc7cc8cc0b8", "fa325d14a5a0", "ab01f9d33994"]}, {"title": "Goldich dissolution series", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldich_dissolution_series", "revid": 1360679337, "sent_ids": ["0caf56abe9ed"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q848628", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-found-031", "soil-found-029"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-found-031": "ordering pass", "soil-form-135": "ordering pass", "soil-found-029": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 125, "position": 4}
{"title": "Goldich dissolution series", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Major", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Goldich dissolution series", "definition": {"text": "A ranking of how fast common igneous minerals weather at Earth's surface, with minerals formed at higher temperatures and pressures less stable than those formed at lower ones.", "sent_ids": ["540ce55aab30"]}, "facts": [{"text": "S. S. Goldich derived it in 1938 after studying soil profiles and their parent rocks.", "sent_ids": ["c077c20a10c6"]}, {"text": "Mafic minerals, rich in iron and magnesium, are less stable than felsic, silica-rich ones; the order echoes Bowen's reaction series.", "sent_ids": ["a31ba2e9b5bb", "1b0dfb40d51f"]}, {"text": "Apatite is the quickest common igneous mineral to weather and quartz the slowest, a difference that can span millions of years.", "sent_ids": ["dcc01ff0b787", "1fcc6f9740e5"]}, {"text": "Quartz, least prone to weathering, is very common as a residual mineral in residual soils, while basaltic minerals weather relatively quickly.", "sent_ids": ["571b92b432f7", "31abc8c27a2d"]}, {"text": "It covers intrinsic mineral properties; extrinsic factors such as climate, through water-to-rock ratio, pH and alkalinity, also control weathering rate.", "sent_ids": ["c15798fa328e", "5cf96ea435d8", "0d035d86e3bc"]}, {"text": "Mafic rocks weather to serpentine, Mg- and Ca-rich clays and iron oxides; felsic rocks to clays such as kaolinite, montmorillonite and illite.", "sent_ids": ["9cd788268e8d", "72326dbeef19"]}, {"text": "It is applied to lithosequences, soils compared under similar weathering conditions so that differences reflect their parent minerals.", "sent_ids": ["3fcea8a56a97", "000040a975e7"]}, {"text": "Experiments by White and Brantley (2003) found K-feldspar and plagioclase weather at similar rates, so the series may not hold for all weathering processes.", "sent_ids": ["411be4c3a20e", "7e10e7dfa0ef", "b12bc1477483"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-chem-186", "soil-found-058", "soil-form-135"], "related": ["soil-chem-086", "soil-form-087", "soil-chem-193"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": "S:dcc01ff0b787 and S:50f24e2364f1/S:917b23df2e8e give mineral lifetimes as '105.48 years', '108.59 years' etc.; the extraction lost the superscripts (10^5.48 etc.), so the values were left out of the facts.", "part": "Soil chemistry", "chapter": "Soil mineralogy", "id": "soil-chem-187", "score": 10.22, "score_parts": {"importance": 3.219, "weight": 3, "tests": 2, "pages": 2.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Goldich dissolution series", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldich_dissolution_series", "revid": 1360679337, "sent_ids": ["c077c20a10c6", "a31ba2e9b5bb", "1b0dfb40d51f", "dcc01ff0b787", "1fcc6f9740e5", "c15798fa328e", "5cf96ea435d8", "0d035d86e3bc", "9cd788268e8d", "72326dbeef19", "3fcea8a56a97", "000040a975e7", "411be4c3a20e", "7e10e7dfa0ef", "b12bc1477483", "540ce55aab30"]}, {"title": "Quartz", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quartz", "revid": 1383182792, "sent_ids": ["571b92b432f7"]}, {"title": "Soil", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil", "revid": 1385366388, "sent_ids": ["31abc8c27a2d"]}], "events": [{"date_text": "in 1938", "date_ma": 1.2e-05, "sent_ids": ["c077c20a10c6"]}], "flags": [], "qid": "Q5580168", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-chem-186", "soil-found-058"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-chem-186": "ordering pass", "soil-found-058": "ordering pass", "soil-form-135": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 126, "position": 1}
{"title": "Powder diffraction", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Major", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Powder diffraction", "definition": {"text": "A technique that uses X-ray, neutron or electron diffraction on powdered or microcrystalline samples to characterize the structure of materials.", "sent_ids": ["b62ff787fffa"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Each crystalline solid gives a distinctive pattern; line positions, set by lattice spacings, and their intensities act as a fingerprint.", "sent_ids": ["633d4f8339dc", "29fbff96f726"]}, {"text": "A mixture such as a soil sample shows several superposed patterns, from which the relative amounts of each phase can be found.", "sent_ids": ["971b91d75dca", "49ed8833305f"]}, {"text": "Diffraction occurs when radiation with a wavelength comparable to atomic spacings reflects off planes of atoms and interferes constructively.", "sent_ids": ["dcacdfe58c2e"]}, {"text": "Bragg's law, nλ = 2d sin θ, links the wavelength λ, the lattice spacing d and the glancing angle θ; the Braggs proposed it in 1913.", "sent_ids": ["ba7b0076ca47", "3a6280ddbc31"]}, {"text": "It has long been used to identify and classify minerals, and works on nearly any material for which a reference pattern exists.", "sent_ids": ["e2a3e15ed268"]}, {"text": "The 2021 Powder Diffraction File held over 1,047,661 reference materials.", "sent_ids": ["204abc192f45"]}, {"text": "Its strengths are simple sample preparation and fast measurement; it mainly identifies phases rather than solving unknown structures.", "sent_ids": ["473fbac4a977", "522616831965", "6f76ed974c9a"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-found-029", "soil-chem-184"], "related": ["soil-chem-082", "soil-chem-189", "soil-chem-184", "soil-chem-079"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil chemistry", "chapter": "Soil mineralogy", "id": "soil-chem-188", "score": 8.78, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.197, "weight": 3, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Powder diffraction", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Powder_diffraction", "revid": 1331154464, "sent_ids": ["633d4f8339dc", "29fbff96f726", "971b91d75dca", "49ed8833305f", "e2a3e15ed268", "204abc192f45", "473fbac4a977", "522616831965", "6f76ed974c9a", "b62ff787fffa"]}, {"title": "Bragg's law", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bragg's_law", "revid": 1361393274, "sent_ids": ["dcacdfe58c2e", "ba7b0076ca47", "3a6280ddbc31"]}], "events": [{"date_text": "in 1913", "date_ma": 3.7e-05, "sent_ids": ["3a6280ddbc31"]}], "flags": [], "qid": "Q376044", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-found-029", "soil-chem-079"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-found-029": "ordering pass", "soil-chem-184": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 127, "position": 4}
{"title": "X-ray fluorescence", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "X-ray fluorescence", "definition": {"text": "The emission of characteristic X-rays from a material excited by high-energy X-rays or gamma rays, whose energies and intensities reveal which elements are present and in what amounts.", "sent_ids": ["11288fb88381", "6fe6cd47584b"]}, "facts": [{"text": "It is a non-destructive technique for elemental analysis used in geochemistry, archaeology and forensic science, among other fields.", "sent_ids": ["4376df92ee03"]}, {"text": "Minerals may be ground and pressed into a tablet or, for accurate work, dissolved in borate glass and cast into a bead.", "sent_ids": ["a8cd77507920", "1266606d6136"]}, {"text": "In pressed mineral powders the finer minerals gather at the surface and mixtures cause absorption effects that depart from theory.", "sent_ids": ["3f4af0edba56", "a0fbeec5d9ce"]}, {"text": "XRF tools are increasingly run with cone penetration test probes for in situ soil measurements.", "sent_ids": ["01356224c110"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-found-029", "soil-chem-184"], "related": ["soil-chem-188", "soil-chem-191"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil chemistry", "chapter": "Soil mineralogy", "id": "soil-chem-189", "score": 7.16, "score_parts": {"importance": 3.584, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "X-ray fluorescence", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X-ray_fluorescence", "revid": 1361269241, "sent_ids": ["4376df92ee03", "a8cd77507920", "1266606d6136", "3f4af0edba56", "a0fbeec5d9ce", "11288fb88381", "6fe6cd47584b"]}, {"title": "Cone penetration test", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cone_penetration_test", "revid": 1327894859, "sent_ids": ["01356224c110"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q898974", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-found-029"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-found-029": "ordering pass", "soil-chem-184": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 127, "position": 5}
{"title": "Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy", "definition": {"text": "A technique that records the infrared absorption or emission spectrum of a solid, liquid or gas over a wide range of wavelengths at once.", "sent_ids": ["d966256b948d", "352bbec671c4"]}, "facts": [{"text": "A mathematical Fourier transform converts the raw data into the spectrum, which gives the method its name.", "sent_ids": ["adf2bafabd10"]}, {"text": "Geologists use it to quantify water and carbon dioxide in minerals and glasses.", "sent_ids": ["a4d4ff378f6c"]}, {"text": "Allophane, which lacks long-range order, is identified by its distinctive infrared spectrum and X-ray diffraction pattern.", "sent_ids": ["03b0147ce96f"]}, {"text": "Infrared spectroscopy is among the methods, with X-ray and electron diffraction, used to study clay minerals.", "sent_ids": ["8df755f8d7c6"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-chem-184"], "related": ["soil-chem-188", "soil-chem-097", "soil-chem-082", "soil-chem-079"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil chemistry", "chapter": "Soil mineralogy", "id": "soil-chem-190", "score": 6.2, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.197, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 2.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fourier-transform_infrared_spectroscopy", "revid": 1372706156, "sent_ids": ["adf2bafabd10", "a4d4ff378f6c", "d966256b948d", "352bbec671c4"]}, {"title": "Allophane", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allophane", "revid": 1345744404, "sent_ids": ["03b0147ce96f"]}, {"title": "Clay mineral", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clay_mineral", "revid": 1370861045, "sent_ids": ["8df755f8d7c6"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q901559", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-chem-079"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-chem-184": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 128, "position": 2}
{"title": "Electron probe microanalysis", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Electron probe microanalysis", "definition": {"text": "A technique that bombards small volumes of a solid with an electron beam and reads the emitted X-rays to determine its chemical composition non-destructively.", "sent_ids": ["ff8fa20d413c", "b906bd3163b7", "594865470c7c"]}, "facts": [{"text": "It shares its principle with the energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy of scanning electron microscopes, but uses a fixed beam and aims at analysis rather than imaging.", "sent_ids": ["12a44f718382"]}, {"text": "It is used mostly by mineralogists and petrologists, revealing chemical zoning from core to rim of a mineral grain that records its history.", "sent_ids": ["8211e66b477e", "3481c0209b3a", "6c1edee95ec1"]}, {"text": "Tephrochronology relies on geochemical fingerprinting of ash, usually with an electron microprobe.", "sent_ids": ["873b240c7514"]}, {"text": "Wavelength-dispersive analysis is limited for light elements such as hydrogen, lithium and beryllium.", "sent_ids": ["45846efd3723"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-chem-189", "soil-found-029"], "related": ["soil-chem-189", "soil-chem-200", "soil-chem-188"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil chemistry", "chapter": "Soil mineralogy", "id": "soil-chem-191", "score": 6.8, "score_parts": {"importance": 3.219, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Electron probe microanalysis", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electron_probe_microanalysis", "revid": 1346219625, "sent_ids": ["12a44f718382", "8211e66b477e", "3481c0209b3a", "6c1edee95ec1", "45846efd3723", "ff8fa20d413c", "b906bd3163b7", "594865470c7c"]}, {"title": "Tephrochronology", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tephrochronology", "revid": 1337190104, "sent_ids": ["873b240c7514"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q911272", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-found-029"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-chem-189": "ordering pass", "soil-found-029": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 128, "position": 1}
{"title": "Environmental magnetism", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Environmental magnetism", "definition": {"text": "The study of how climate, sediment transport, pollution and other environmental influences affect magnetic minerals, using their magnetic properties as proxies.", "sent_ids": ["98fb9c26eb81", "6526e0d6b996"]}, "facts": [{"text": "The main magnetic minerals are iron oxides such as magnetite, maghemite and hematite, and iron sulfides such as greigite and pyrrhotite.", "sent_ids": ["5998751a52f8"]}, {"text": "Magnetic susceptibility and remanence are used to estimate mineral size and composition; measurements are quick and non-invasive.", "sent_ids": ["a3b1f23a4da6", "bcb17686a63a", "cecea61f6536"]}, {"text": "Susceptibility profiles of alternating loess and paleosols in north-central China mapped Quaternary monsoon variations.", "sent_ids": ["6ee99c9e88c9", "902b3db232ce", "8caafcfa79bc"]}, {"text": "Soil magnetism rises when goethite and ferrihydrite are converted to maghemite by heat.", "sent_ids": ["5d18cb3481af"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-found-035"], "related": ["soil-form-111", "soil-chem-104", "soil-chem-111", "soil-form-072", "soil-chem-163", "soil-chem-166"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil chemistry", "chapter": "Soil mineralogy", "id": "soil-chem-192", "score": 3.58, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Environmental magnetism", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Environmental_magnetism", "revid": 1292745194, "sent_ids": ["5998751a52f8", "a3b1f23a4da6", "bcb17686a63a", "cecea61f6536", "6ee99c9e88c9", "902b3db232ce", "8caafcfa79bc", "98fb9c26eb81", "6526e0d6b996"]}, {"title": "Chernozem", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chernozem", "revid": 1381599705, "sent_ids": ["5d18cb3481af"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q5381327", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-found-035"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-found-035": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 128, "position": 5}
{"title": "Heavy mineral", "kind": "Material", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Heavy mineral", "definition": {"text": "A mineral denser than 2.9 g/cm3, usually one of the dense minor components of siliciclastic sediments.", "sent_ids": ["7fca0935b4bd"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Common heavy minerals include zircon, tourmaline, rutile, garnet and magnetite, dense minerals that resist weathering.", "sent_ids": ["a8a6ee164ac3", "52b2e3828c01"]}, {"text": "The relative proportions of heavy minerals, a heavy mineral suite, help reveal the provenance of sediments.", "sent_ids": ["cefaed7ce042", "2f8f28653b56"]}, {"text": "Being minor constituents, they are separated for study in a dense liquid such as bromoform, in a funnel or centrifuge.", "sent_ids": ["994cc8774e87", "49eb9b5e8fd8", "cf720cd2d263"]}, {"text": "Deep weathering concentrates heavy minerals in residual accumulations.", "sent_ids": ["bc6ff6f8b262"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-found-029", "soil-form-136", "soil-found-052"], "related": ["soil-chem-187", "soil-form-087"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil chemistry", "chapter": "Soil mineralogy", "id": "soil-chem-193", "score": 4.0, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 2.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Heavy mineral", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heavy_mineral", "revid": 1321323484, "sent_ids": ["cefaed7ce042", "2f8f28653b56", "994cc8774e87", "49eb9b5e8fd8", "cf720cd2d263", "7fca0935b4bd"]}, {"title": "Sandstone", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandstone", "revid": 1384570234, "sent_ids": ["a8a6ee164ac3", "52b2e3828c01"]}, {"title": "Saprolite", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saprolite", "revid": 1292036048, "sent_ids": ["bc6ff6f8b262"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q5695273", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-found-029", "soil-found-052"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-found-029": "ordering pass", "soil-form-136": "ordering pass", "soil-found-052": "consolidation"}, "unit": 128, "position": 4}
{"title": "Selective extraction of iron oxides", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": null, "definition": {"text": "Chemical extractions used in soil analysis to dissolve particular iron- and aluminium-bearing mineral phases selectively so they can be measured.", "sent_ids": ["79735befb52b", "2a28592f904b"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Acid ammonium oxalate, at pH 3, extracts iron and aluminium from poorly crystalline minerals such as ferrihydrite, from iron(II) minerals such as magnetite, and from organic matter.", "sent_ids": ["2a28592f904b"]}, {"text": "Dithionite with a complexing agent such as citric acid reduces iron(III) oxyhydroxides to soluble iron(II) and removes amorphous iron(III) phases.", "sent_ids": ["79735befb52b"]}, {"text": "Dithionite is a reducing agent with a reduction potential of −0.66 V at pH 7.", "sent_ids": ["eb393e1565f3", "2c5af7a29e59"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-found-035"], "related": ["soil-chem-078", "soil-chem-104", "soil-form-085", "soil-chem-111", "soil-chem-163"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": "Concept passes test 5: described on the Ammonium oxalate and Dithionite pages; neither page warrants its own entry.", "part": "Soil chemistry", "chapter": "Soil mineralogy", "id": "soil-chem-194", "score": 2.58, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Dithionite", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dithionite", "revid": 1386041493, "sent_ids": ["79735befb52b", "eb393e1565f3", "2c5af7a29e59"]}, {"title": "Ammonium oxalate", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ammonium_oxalate", "revid": 1367568958, "sent_ids": ["2a28592f904b"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-found-035", "soil-chem-111"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-found-035": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 128, "position": 3}
{"title": "Birnessite", "kind": "Material", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Birnessite", "definition": {"text": "A hydrous manganese dioxide mineral, nominally MnO2·nH2O, the main manganese mineral at Earth's surface.", "sent_ids": ["0d58b46e675d", "44302d8e747e"]}, "facts": [{"text": "It occurs as fine-grained, poorly crystalline aggregates in soils, sediments, rock coatings such as desert varnish and marine nodules.", "sent_ids": ["44302d8e747e"]}, {"text": "Most natural birnessite is thought to be made by microorganisms, since biological oxidation of Mn(II) is faster than abiotic oxidation.", "sent_ids": ["8c00e8e502ec"]}, {"text": "Vacancies in its layers are balanced by interlayer cations such as calcium, copper, zinc, lead and cadmium.", "sent_ids": ["3370979e347d"]}, {"text": "Manganese oxides can oxidize trivalent chromium in soils to hexavalent chromium.", "sent_ids": ["74f2ce598fd7"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-found-035", "soil-found-029"], "related": ["soil-chem-122", "soil-form-085"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil chemistry", "chapter": "Soil mineralogy", "id": "soil-chem-195", "score": 5.78, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.197, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Birnessite", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birnessite", "revid": 1377206594, "sent_ids": ["44302d8e747e", "8c00e8e502ec", "3370979e347d", "0d58b46e675d"]}, {"title": "Chromium", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chromium", "revid": 1385990348, "sent_ids": ["74f2ce598fd7"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q3640357", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-found-035"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-found-035": "ordering pass", "soil-found-029": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 126, "position": 5}
{"title": "Glauconite", "kind": "Material", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Glauconite", "definition": {"text": "A green iron potassium phyllosilicate of the mica group, very friable and with very low resistance to weathering.", "sent_ids": ["5898d3f6ccf8"]}, "facts": [{"text": "It forms in place in marine settings, commonly under low oxygen, and indicates continental shelf deposits laid down slowly.", "sent_ids": ["d3e56bf9adf6", "27ac93643170", "c009ea3cfd04"]}, {"text": "As the main component of greensand it is a common potassium source in fertilizers and is used to adjust soil pH.", "sent_ids": ["259d204e57de"]}, {"text": "Greensand is low in potassium, so it pays to transport it only short distances to fields.", "sent_ids": ["532d90cc1a71"]}, {"text": "Konstantin Glinka's 1896 master's thesis dealt with glauconite's origin, composition and weathering.", "sent_ids": ["20018d2676f2"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-found-033", "soil-found-052"], "related": ["soil-fert-009", "soil-chem-094", "soil-agri-038", "soil-chem-135"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil chemistry", "chapter": "Soil mineralogy", "id": "soil-chem-196", "score": 8.39, "score_parts": {"importance": 4.394, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 2.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Glauconite", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glauconite", "revid": 1368879765, "sent_ids": ["d3e56bf9adf6", "27ac93643170", "c009ea3cfd04", "259d204e57de", "5898d3f6ccf8"]}, {"title": "Konstantin Glinka", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konstantin_Glinka", "revid": 1378084715, "sent_ids": ["20018d2676f2"]}, {"title": "Potash", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Potash", "revid": 1385549092, "sent_ids": ["532d90cc1a71"]}], "events": [{"date_text": "In 1896", "date_ma": 5.4e-05, "sent_ids": ["20018d2676f2"]}], "flags": [], "qid": "Q423034", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-found-033", "soil-chem-135"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-found-033": "ordering pass", "soil-found-052": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 126, "position": 3}
{"title": "Jarosite", "kind": "Material", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Jarosite", "definition": {"text": "A basic hydrous sulfate of potassium and ferric iron, KFe3(SO4)2(OH)6, formed by the oxidation of iron sulfides.", "sent_ids": ["16676548e7b4", "87d7bcdd5d08"]}, "facts": [{"text": "It is common in acid mine drainage and acid sulfate soil environments.", "sent_ids": ["04dd71ddb433"]}, {"text": "When it forms from pyrite oxidation in clays, its potassium comes mainly from illite or K-feldspar.", "sent_ids": ["dffd8781b669"]}, {"text": "With schwertmannite it holds retained acidity, releasing it slowly through hydrolysis of Fe3+ and Al3+; the WRB horizon suffix j marks their accumulation.", "sent_ids": ["d178a20b1636", "1e0ebb58a9a8"]}, {"text": "Rovers have found it on Mars, a sign of strongly oxidizing surface conditions.", "sent_ids": ["512436525e8d", "75333b7f1338"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-found-036"], "related": ["soil-chem-198", "soil-chem-031", "soil-chem-033", "soil-chem-030", "soil-chem-113", "soil-chem-134"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil chemistry", "chapter": "Soil mineralogy", "id": "soil-chem-197", "score": 7.89, "score_parts": {"importance": 3.892, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 2.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Jarosite", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jarosite", "revid": 1380688090, "sent_ids": ["04dd71ddb433", "dffd8781b669", "512436525e8d", "75333b7f1338", "16676548e7b4", "87d7bcdd5d08"]}, {"title": "Acid sulfate soil", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acid_sulfate_soil", "revid": 1383142842, "sent_ids": ["d178a20b1636"]}, {"title": "Soil horizon", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_horizon", "revid": 1385480933, "sent_ids": ["1e0ebb58a9a8"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q411168", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-found-036", "soil-chem-030"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-found-036": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 126, "position": 4}
{"title": "Schwertmannite", "kind": "Material", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Schwertmannite", "definition": {"text": "An iron oxyhydroxysulfate mineral, ideally Fe8O8(OH)6(SO4)·nH2O, usually seen as brownish-yellow crusts.", "sent_ids": ["0916807d7987", "ed57c734a62c"]}, "facts": [{"text": "It forms in iron-rich, acidic sulfate waters with a pH of 2 to 4.", "sent_ids": ["65578cfa1a22"]}, {"text": "It is commonly an orange precipitate in streams and lakes affected by acid mine drainage.", "sent_ids": ["7a4f55571f70"]}, {"text": "It is central to iron-sulfur geochemistry in the acid sulfate soils of coastal lowlands.", "sent_ids": ["664b472a2448"]}, {"text": "First described in 1994, it is named for the soil scientist Udo Schwertmann of the Technical University of Munich.", "sent_ids": ["cf6ef205403b"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-found-036"], "related": ["soil-chem-197", "soil-chem-111", "soil-chem-033", "soil-chem-030", "soil-chem-163"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil chemistry", "chapter": "Soil mineralogy", "id": "soil-chem-198", "score": 4.2, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.197, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Schwertmannite", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schwertmannite", "revid": 1315391539, "sent_ids": ["65578cfa1a22", "7a4f55571f70", "664b472a2448", "cf6ef205403b", "0916807d7987", "ed57c734a62c"]}], "events": [{"date_text": "in 1994", "date_ma": -4.4e-05, "sent_ids": ["cf6ef205403b"]}], "flags": [], "qid": "Q410325", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-found-036", "soil-chem-030"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-found-036": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 127, "position": 1}
{"title": "Zeolite", "kind": "Material", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Zeolite", "definition": {"text": "A group of microporous, crystalline aluminosilicate minerals widely used as adsorbents and catalysts.", "sent_ids": ["ec825b6c1831"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Their negatively charged aluminium centres need accompanying cations, which can be exchanged, giving zeolites ion-exchange properties.", "sent_ids": ["6c79226a600b", "ed62e3368de3"]}, {"text": "Over 40 naturally occurring frameworks are known among 253 identified by December 2018.", "sent_ids": ["b6844bf9d4b2"]}, {"text": "Altered volcanic glass has formed zeolite deposits.", "sent_ids": ["adaf102733c3"]}, {"text": "Much material sold as zeolite is ground volcanic tuff holding little zeolite, used for building stone, aggregate, cement and soil conditioners.", "sent_ids": ["b89d904608f6", "032047dc4b4c"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-found-031"], "related": ["soil-chem-200", "soil-chem-100", "soil-found-032", "soil-chem-046", "soil-chem-145"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil chemistry", "chapter": "Soil mineralogy", "id": "soil-chem-199", "score": 9.57, "score_parts": {"importance": 5.991, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Zeolite", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zeolite", "revid": 1384138297, "sent_ids": ["6c79226a600b", "ed62e3368de3", "b6844bf9d4b2", "b89d904608f6", "032047dc4b4c", "ec825b6c1831"]}, {"title": "Volcanic glass", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volcanic_glass", "revid": 1353838505, "sent_ids": ["adaf102733c3"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q181332", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-found-031", "soil-chem-046"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-found-031": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 125, "position": 3}
{"title": "Volcanic glass", "kind": "Material", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Volcanic glass", "definition": {"text": "The amorphous, non-crystalline product of rapidly cooled magma.", "sent_ids": ["8397a1929483", "fcdff97870ee"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Lacking a crystal structure, it reacts easily with water and breaks down over time, forming new minerals.", "sent_ids": ["9eeff88a653a", "04aa22a5d242", "7e5c62eb694c"]}, {"text": "Allophane forms by weathering of volcanic glass and feldspars, and devitrified glass in ash beds becomes bentonite clay.", "sent_ids": ["823cae641cdd", "48c91e54de2f"]}, {"text": "Vitric Andosols, with limited soil formation, still contain much volcanic glass.", "sent_ids": ["0cbf60f4e0d4"]}, {"text": "Soils with duripans are often found in volcanic areas and show evidence of ash or volcanic glass.", "sent_ids": ["757b29ae088e"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-form-135", "soil-form-152", "soil-found-029", "soil-form-087", "soil-found-052"], "related": ["soil-form-096", "soil-class-119", "soil-chem-097", "soil-chem-199"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil chemistry", "chapter": "Soil mineralogy", "id": "soil-chem-200", "score": 9.55, "score_parts": {"importance": 4.97, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 2.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Volcanic glass", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volcanic_glass", "revid": 1353838505, "sent_ids": ["9eeff88a653a", "04aa22a5d242", "7e5c62eb694c", "8397a1929483", "fcdff97870ee"]}, {"title": "Allophane", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allophane", "revid": 1345744404, "sent_ids": ["823cae641cdd"]}, {"title": "Andosol", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andosol", "revid": 1358126439, "sent_ids": ["0cbf60f4e0d4"]}, {"title": "Bentonite", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bentonite", "revid": 1385640109, "sent_ids": ["48c91e54de2f"]}, {"title": "Duripan", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duripan", "revid": 1338570446, "sent_ids": ["757b29ae088e"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q107749", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-form-087", "soil-found-052"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-form-135": "ordering pass", "soil-form-152": "ordering pass", "soil-found-029": "ordering pass", "soil-form-087": "consolidation", "soil-found-052": "consolidation"}, "unit": 126, "position": 2}
